---
import Chapter from '../Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../Figure.astro';
import FigMigration from '../figures/FigMigration.astro';
---

<Chapter id="migration" num="10" kicker="Post-quantum migration" title="Migrating is traversing an edge">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      As each domain migrates at its own pace, traditional, hybrid and post-quantum domains will
      coexist for years. Because <strong>one key is one node</strong> and each key carries its
      level, a mixed path can be read on the graph edge by edge, with no extra mechanism.
      <span class="sn sn--key"
        ><b>Key idea</b> A domain never accepts a route through a certification authority below its cryptographic floor, anchor included, whether the other domains migrate at the same time or not.</span
      >
    </p>
    <p>
      A CA that changes algorithm holds two keys, linked by the certificates of RFC 9810: new with
      old, and old with new. Each is an edge with its own cost, and the two directions do not cost
      the same, because the return certificate carries the ML-DSA signature, which is the larger
      part.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure num={13} class="figure--text" caption="A CA that migrates from ECDSA P-384 to ML-DSA-65. Entering the post-quantum key takes 2,056 octets and going back 3,406. Both edges touch the traditional key.">
    <FigMigration />
  </Figure>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Under a strict floor, then, a link certificate signed with the traditional key does not lead
      to the new one. The certificates that the migrating CA signs become admissible again only when a CA that
      meets the floor certifies its post-quantum key directly, and Govern can announce that before
      the receiver raises the floor. The floor also binds the receiver’s own branch, anchor
      included: a domain cannot demand of others a level its own chain does not have.
      <span class="sn"
        ><b>A list of floors.</b> With <span class="m">τ<sub>B</sub></span> = ⟨(<code>post_quantum</code>, 0),
        (<code>hybrid</code>, 0)⟩ the receiver asks for the strongest without running out of
        routes, and Audit records under which floor each path was admitted.</span
      >
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="key">
    A receiver governs its own migration simply by raising <span class="math">τ<sub>d</sub></span>, and it does so without reissuing a single certificate.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <p class="small muted">
      Using ML-DSA certificates does not make the channel post-quantum, since the thesis acts on the
      certificates and on the path decision while the key exchange and the handshake signatures of the
      channel are separate layers.
    </p>
  </div>
</Chapter>

<style>
  .muted {
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }
</style>
